9fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-139058" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={3}^{t},\\,y={e}^{t},\\,t\\in [0,3].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=3.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({3}^{t})={3}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{3}^{t}\\sqrt{{({3}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{3}^{t}\\sqrt{{3}^{2t}{ln}^{2}⁡3+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{3}^{t}\\sqrt{{9}^{t}{ln}^{2}⁡3+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269202 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ee47220-a4e2-5159-8d37-677dd4043a6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267417} {:answer/id #uuid "9067b7fc-a784-5978-a465-6f6a6dc216b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267429} {:answer/id #uuid "adc5904c-70e0-54de-b29f-39558bf28f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267716} {:answer/id #uuid "c54b2e91-b69e-516f-841a-2f92146c6439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267912} {:answer/id #uuid "ce0b6ea6-3030-5898-b7bd-3d23d852d40a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9067b7fc-a784-5978-a465-6f6a6dc216b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267429} :answer-field/id #uuid "273204cb-906b-5113-89a6-a83acb7746a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3bd931f3-299c-5a93-bcdc-18fadb4baee0" :question/math-academy-id "q-139063" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y={e}^{t},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269227 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "206f844b-a9aa-543a-b13a-3a4acc9da6a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266430} {:answer/id #uuid "9605b247-8a44-51d4-bd86-9cbc5c513452" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267482} {:answer/id #uuid "9d07c8ea-8eb0-51e5-9466-d659439c9524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267555} {:answer/id #uuid "9e8a4161-e6e2-529a-abea-93d855445919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267572} {:answer/id #uuid "fe6d2f84-0611-5fed-a6aa-de2e286504b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268384}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e8a4161-e6e2-529a-abea-93d855445919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267572} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e74f88c-8d6d-562e-9858-e61dad89652f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0761cfb5-82f2-5ff1-afcd-0792aa3de132" :question/math-academy-id "q-139475" :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 14\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ Equivalently, it is the acute angle between $x$ and any non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,2\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot (-3)+(-1)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 3}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{6}\\sqrt{18}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269228 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06ab9f5c-f9b0-5dd6-918a-bb731247e079" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266201} {:answer/id #uuid "0a2f65bb-4526-584f-856f-27215c1529ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266236} {:answer/id #uuid "21b515ce-8113-511c-85a0-cd526375bb7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266446} {:answer/id #uuid "39bf3cee-b002-50a4-ac7c-283a66da8f06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266653} {:answer/id #uuid "90a4c538-7615-5efa-b16c-ec8c0f84764b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90a4c538-7615-5efa-b16c-ec8c0f84764b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267432} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1bfa4e5-ee67-5872-a778-8cac048ea831" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a3f9a9b-9c5c-5941-8f74-a42675358e73" :question/math-academy-id "q-139477" :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ Equivalently, it is the acute angle between $x$ and any non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,2\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{0\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269229 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b1719c2-cbaf-5a8a-8ad8-6d7f32d5f2cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266244} {:answer/id #uuid "99fb8f73-5ddb-5794-8d92-72d920d52b65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267537} {:answer/id #uuid "d47ccd6e-8624-5b7c-9993-a19ea7edac40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268040} {:answer/id #uuid "e7478d11-b7f3-5a15-be65-b95013b60046" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268194} {:answer/id #uuid "f487078a-66cc-52f3-bdfd-0b205ae902e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7478d11-b7f3-5a15-be65-b95013b60046" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268194} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd83e725-b731-57f9-a6b5-a6f89fe153b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "12bd1922-22fd-5b46-aa73-11894915ba39" :question/math-academy-id "q-139478" :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 27\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{18}\\begin{bmatrix}27 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ Equivalently, it is the acute angle between $x$ and any non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,2\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{(-6)\\cdot (-3)+(-3)\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 6}{\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{3}^{2}+{6}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{\\sqrt{54}\\sqrt{54}} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{54} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269233 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b09663d-300e-5ee5-8466-82d21294665d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{22.73}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266523} {:answer/id #uuid "4e24ae49-2513-5475-9c72-110a755c76e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{24.11}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266838} {:answer/id #uuid "74ed45fa-c197-5e50-b36d-5a3685a5918c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{27.53}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267170} {:answer/id #uuid "ad689cad-1bb7-5646-b2b3-5e7e409b82ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{19.24}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267710} {:answer/id #uuid "f758adb1-2b16-59b2-ae75-525dfd04d76f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{24.09}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f758adb1-2b16-59b2-ae75-525dfd04d76f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{24.09}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268337} :answer-field/id #uuid "743f8294-a0bd-590c-8886-d9cce43ee982" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27ff07db-fdfd-5460-9a6e-b2c28eedff32" :question/math-academy-id "q-139513" :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRound the answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we get the following projection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 8\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{24}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 11\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ Equivalently, it is the acute angle between $x$ and any non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,2\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}x}{‖x‖‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{9}\\sqrt{30}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{3\\sqrt{30}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}})≈{24.09}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269234 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a09b02d-8bbe-5d10-a4f9-370bef55e764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{21.83}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266234} {:answer/id #uuid "60cc524b-fcb3-5eda-a2ef-972dc00b4dd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{20.02}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266999} {:answer/id #uuid "d0230632-643e-5176-9cfd-33172a36de8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{19.47}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268005} {:answer/id #uuid "e4a14d53-aae0-5887-9f9d-89614f80206d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{17.36}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268164} {:answer/id #uuid "e6ffd20f-b627-5d13-90b1-7aab917e72f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{18.88}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0230632-643e-5176-9cfd-33172a36de8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{19.47}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268005} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1122a16-f57b-5b34-8e12-fe56444b19bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a21d579a-3fab-5e90-bfa1-e3d7308d1aaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-139514" :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRound the answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we get the following projection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{8} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ Equivalently, it is the acute angle between $x$ and any non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}x}{‖x‖‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 1+(-5)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(-5)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{54}\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{9\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}})≈{19.47}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272015 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b5f320c-678c-588f-97ca-59e7f48ab67f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270224} {:answer/id #uuid "e976cf72-85c5-52fb-8497-4a6b96dccdff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271311} {:answer/id #uuid "e9961cc7-e783-51ec-bc2d-47d357514968" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271315} {:answer/id #uuid "f4309c69-9db1-5dc4-aa51-0b57a127e2e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271410} {:answer/id #uuid "fb64b1dd-5faf-5d58-a38e-7a3c3bf0ddab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e976cf72-85c5-52fb-8497-4a6b96dccdff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271311} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a6aca89-aa2e-56f0-bbd1-0f5adf3139d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "472ad991-cd49-54f3-aa78-cfe11b836a0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-140053" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 15 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}a & 5 \\\\ b & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the value of  $\\frac{a}{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$\n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 15 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 15 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 15 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{5}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{3}{1}=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272016 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "434859e0-5b7a-524b-8296-6aa41ec7669b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269876} {:answer/id #uuid "a69d7ffb-2348-53e1-80d7-c6077d248e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270760} {:answer/id #uuid "d6221acf-c2d5-5c9e-a305-28c990e86077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271145} {:answer/id #uuid "ee44e5bf-b05a-55c0-9931-0fe57e147573" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271359} {:answer/id #uuid "fd1e6265-66d6-501e-844e-d925219ae749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee44e5bf-b05a-55c0-9931-0fe57e147573" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271359} :answer-field/id #uuid "aaf04191-f697-50a2-b21d-5e1af7e197d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0f3dda67-4437-5ed9-8f86-30b8b0141d47" :question/math-academy-id "q-140054" :question/problem "Find matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=B,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix and $B=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -6 \\\\ 8 & -7\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.** We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix $B$ using its characteristic equation $det(B-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}7-\\lambda  & -6 \\\\ 8 & -7-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(-7-\\lambda )-(-6)\\cdot 8 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-49+48 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $B.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-1)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -6 \\\\ 8 & -7\\end{bmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -6 \\\\ 8 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-1I & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -6 \\\\ 8 & -7\\end{bmatrix}-1\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -6 \\\\ 8 & -8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{4}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272046 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b92c754-eb13-5d62-8988-5c9cd5b46d9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+1)}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269662} {:answer/id #uuid "2d6d3379-d6ad-5655-999a-1273f57649fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269678} {:answer/id #uuid "71c32ce2-abcd-589e-9ee2-bf0011fa0aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270278} {:answer/id #uuid "9f2d8b7d-7c0f-5acc-b9f8-e53fbc017c17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270680} {:answer/id #uuid "fd66c5fe-ca7f-51da-b3eb-be1db1ee6189" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-1)}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d6d3379-d6ad-5655-999a-1273f57649fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269678} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd33067a-85fe-5634-91e6-8571e5edc593" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "92dac4ab-f152-5947-b5c0-1c9fee1af33c" :question/math-academy-id "q-140299" :question/problem "Rewrite the expression \n\n$$\n({x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4})-1\n$$\n\nas the difference between a perfect square and a constant." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a)^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& {x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\\n&= {x}^{2}-x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\\n&= {x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\\n&= {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& ({x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4})-1 \\\\\n&= {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272049 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22ede277-799a-55e8-9b60-af750e6d9683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269573} {:answer/id #uuid "4444677c-9e00-5f98-a391-79ca6f45abf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{45}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269887} {:answer/id #uuid "6bcdd33e-ce22-52be-b791-8af1cf89a3aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270228} {:answer/id #uuid "7a4464e9-793a-55ca-b8a0-e181e8b930fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{13}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270351} {:answer/id #uuid "97c0b932-9e24-536a-b503-99a9707140ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270604}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22ede277-799a-55e8-9b60-af750e6d9683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269573} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f3033a7-b9ba-57f5-af91-a381b9582063" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d192e29-3a22-5afd-a4d3-7c411bdd4923" :question/math-academy-id "q-140310" :question/problem "Rewrite the expression ${x}^{2}-7x+\\frac{5}{4}$ as the sum of the perfect square and a constant." :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+\\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $\\frac{5}{4}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-11.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272050 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1daac637-c579-566b-9ddd-50d696edd413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{19}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269515} {:answer/id #uuid "816f1d51-1a67-509b-b224-cd6cad7dcf18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270389} {:answer/id #uuid "b18be9f5-ed01-5730-afaa-dda65510daf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{13}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270857} {:answer/id #uuid "ef1ae62c-ea8e-5f99-8de6-eeacf0d16dbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271368} {:answer/id #uuid "f19c21e4-ddf0-5020-8192-bab7ff3be30f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{17}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271389}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f19c21e4-ddf0-5020-8192-bab7ff3be30f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{17}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271389} :answer-field/id #uuid "db721d2d-f4f1-5277-b1c8-6796e6e18403" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "282ce0db-5af9-5d46-bb3e-cd818faa2d16" :question/math-academy-id "q-140312" :question/problem "Rewrite the expression ${x}^{2}-7x+8$ as the sum of the perfect square and a constant." :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+8\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $8$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+8\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+8\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{17}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272051 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f09a1b1-4afd-52a0-aec7-18dc56d5c758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269404} {:answer/id #uuid "3ce3252b-e84e-5e60-99f3-bbd7072bf8d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269824} {:answer/id #uuid "694d853a-178a-51e8-a64c-ad72b3df2069" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270202} {:answer/id #uuid "875c45f2-fe33-5ece-b850-fbf8978aff10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270441} {:answer/id #uuid "c48d330b-c730-5cb2-bdd8-cd369488a7d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "694d853a-178a-51e8-a64c-ad72b3df2069" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270202} :answer-field/id #uuid "fffae713-7eba-52f7-984e-d331b4bcb2a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8eec02ed-0572-53d4-a456-e968589fe066" :question/math-academy-id "q-140314" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 9 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIf the matrix $A$ above is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}5-\\lambda  & 9 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -8-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -6 & -6 & -4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-4-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}5-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ -4 & -8-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-4-\\lambda )((5-\\lambda )(-8-\\lambda )+36) & =0 \\\\ (-4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -4) & =0 \\\\ -(\\lambda +4)(\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -1) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2}(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ (simple root) and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ (double root).\n\nLet's now find the dimension of the eigenspace corresponding to each eigenvalue.\n\n- First, we consider the simple root ${\\lambda }_{1}=1.$ Since the root is simple, the dimension of the eigenspace ${V}_{1}$ corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{1})=1.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the double root ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4.$ Let's find the dimension of the eigenspace ${V}_{-4}$ corresponding to this eigenvalue. To do this, we row-reduce the matrix $A-(-4)I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 9 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}-(-4)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 9 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{3}+(\\frac{4}{9}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). This implies that we will have $3-1=2$ linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{-4})=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ is diagonalizable, since\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{1})+\\text{dim}({V}_{-4})=1+2=3.\\,✓\n$$\n\nFinally, a corresponding diagonal matrix is \n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272058 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "071c98e7-f045-5699-97e6-8ca98ff4c425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{89}{4}-{(x+14)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269336} {:answer/id #uuid "0b6551d7-ff9d-5fcb-9ba8-47eb76d9737a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269367} {:answer/id #uuid "92214c5a-0fb3-5f9c-b2fd-8c3a954013bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "59-{(x+14)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270546} {:answer/id #uuid "b1f6b651-8575-5bc8-a027-fe8e7ce11c40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270861} {:answer/id #uuid "d730e81c-c1fd-5d3a-9ed2-255c3dad533b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b6551d7-ff9d-5fcb-9ba8-47eb76d9737a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269367} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e101ba8-4e79-5217-9a66-13f1e2c2b19e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f049fbc-7296-56e3-bb5f-3d51d2204d7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-140330" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}+7x+10)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+7x+10\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+10\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $10$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}+7x+10) & =-({(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272059 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00259cbe-d8e5-5392-8381-04524c3d0b22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6-{(x-2)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269259} {:answer/id #uuid "260e8dc3-9c29-57ea-b43b-c48d36a9f216" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}-{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269607} {:answer/id #uuid "59ab4b2b-b9b2-5933-a43e-16332e47f135" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}-{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270066} {:answer/id #uuid "6ccf35f0-4836-5526-b487-39bc207dbcf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}-{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270233} {:answer/id #uuid "b357bb45-c3bf-5ad2-ab72-033565c17bd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{4}-{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "260e8dc3-9c29-57ea-b43b-c48d36a9f216" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}-{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269607} :answer-field/id #uuid "66fc580c-7644-50ba-93bc-29818b02080f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "793139fd-bc0b-52b9-b28f-1f9a809139b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-140332" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}-x-2)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-x-2\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x-2\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $2$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}-x-2) & =-({(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}-{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272060 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e0b9d03-98ee-5f65-8899-eb0ca3293f69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}-{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269682} {:answer/id #uuid "51675449-e3ed-5d32-a5a1-dc181d745b38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{161}{4}-{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269997} {:answer/id #uuid "64001e55-d244-54a8-918a-a1c0059b44a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}-{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270154} {:answer/id #uuid "88d86ace-1240-59e6-93cf-c53adca2f146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61-{(y+18)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270450} {:answer/id #uuid "e002a466-5bda-58b6-b497-ea8df55b4790" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}-{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e0b9d03-98ee-5f65-8899-eb0ca3293f69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}-{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269682} :answer-field/id #uuid "19597342-b978-5e00-8333-b2a8755a3138" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271554}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6187e130-749f-5a82-add1-d28e2491c294" :question/math-academy-id "q-140333" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({y}^{2}+9y+20)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+9y+20\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y+20\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $20$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y+{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+20\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+20\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({y}^{2}+9y+20) & =-({(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272061 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b5aff3f-b7d6-5b54-95af-f9f916b0fafc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}-{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269366} {:answer/id #uuid "24adc6aa-bdb9-5bd9-a841-ad28766373fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}-{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269591} {:answer/id #uuid "ca09234d-0fa0-5ea3-b9b3-7fb223f45eec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}-{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271043} {:answer/id #uuid "cb958131-d7ba-5ecd-80c2-45a2252fec88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6-{(x+3)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271056} {:answer/id #uuid "df03a294-16be-5d8a-857d-ba03d3d7b876" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}-{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271225}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df03a294-16be-5d8a-857d-ba03d3d7b876" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}-{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271225} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e8cfdf1-fed6-5630-8b17-89230bdc0f27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0d1304a-bb22-58d1-a96a-e8e13a86ee0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-140337" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}+3x+3)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+3x+3\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+3\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $3$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}+3x+3) & =-({(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}-{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272062 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ca67b8d-08c3-5756-8356-6307d581f0f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}-{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269958} {:answer/id #uuid "68d03655-8b8a-586c-af0d-61d3ff3b9222" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{4}-{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270198} {:answer/id #uuid "ac18e720-115f-5feb-9e45-b7fece57b20a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}-{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270804} {:answer/id #uuid "b09bfde5-2d06-51d1-ba5b-e32c34c0e17b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{53}{4}-{(x+10)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270845} {:answer/id #uuid "df69391d-37bd-5f43-ba1f-a9d2a6f64153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32-{(x+10)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ca67b8d-08c3-5756-8356-6307d581f0f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}-{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269958} :answer-field/id #uuid "30b139f9-5928-5334-bd52-294f57457f52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "90626d5b-7cd2-5adf-91b6-e10a0ab89eaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-140356" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}+5x+7)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+5x+7\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}x+7\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $7$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}x+{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+7\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+7\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}+5x+7) & =-({(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}-{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272070 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25399f39-cd1a-5142-83fd-2faed5a58382" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269599} {:answer/id #uuid "2c113b7e-7f2d-50c2-84be-19bb33799b5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x+\\frac{5}{3})}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269664} {:answer/id #uuid "55abb69f-b764-531c-95eb-eeec736810ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x+\\frac{5}{3})}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270028} {:answer/id #uuid "d6232d29-c295-5652-a6f9-4789848d3505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271146} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5d1b87-4d59-56d8-ac27-ed5cb43e9203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x+\\frac{10}{3})}^{2}-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25399f39-cd1a-5142-83fd-2faed5a58382" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269599} :answer-field/id #uuid "286cef5c-d3e8-5bea-859b-e4618279b6d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0e4b3efa-abe1-5a91-86fa-e6dadf579120" :question/math-academy-id "q-140375" :question/problem "Rewrite $6{x}^{2}+10x-\\frac{11}{6}$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $6$. So, we take out a factor of $6$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n6({x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{3}x-\\frac{11}{36})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{3}x-\\frac{11}{36}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{6}\\cdot x-\\frac{11}{36}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $\\frac{11}{36}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{6}\\cdot x+{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{11}{36}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{11}{36}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression::\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{3}x-\\frac{11}{36}) & =6({(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =6{(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6{(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272081 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d854445-4b9d-5457-8fdd-3e9aafaf84c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{4}-{(y-10)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269970} {:answer/id #uuid "6c18b098-7360-5163-a283-eff16352c701" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{4}-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270231} {:answer/id #uuid "89f2f572-b7c1-5dea-a169-978a09aab668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}+{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270461} {:answer/id #uuid "aa2bcef6-9c81-5bea-950e-d8b1ddb76848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20-{(y-10)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270787} {:answer/id #uuid "ed555ea6-dfea-51d4-876e-75d752688105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed555ea6-dfea-51d4-876e-75d752688105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271354} :answer-field/id #uuid "880bee47-a8c3-50a6-937c-697f411c6825" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "afc8b4da-a3f1-5590-a7d5-e7a094b395bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-140410" :question/problem "Rewrite $-{y}^{2}+5y-5$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1$. So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({y}^{2}-5y+5)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-5y+5\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}y+5\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $5$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}y+{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}+5y-5 & =-({(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272090 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13324668-ce20-5ef2-a468-80b0d3559702" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{86.22}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269438} {:answer/id #uuid "20612eaf-6f72-5c17-b500-3e91c9c59bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{75.28}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269540} {:answer/id #uuid "6c956be0-6107-50ca-9fbb-b6a515d4b585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{83.01}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270232} {:answer/id #uuid "ddd29c40-21e0-51ac-bccc-b59d130e6b0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{65.86}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271217} {:answer/id #uuid "e24ab0a8-78b8-52e7-9bfc-1c0a57398dec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{72.45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c956be0-6107-50ca-9fbb-b6a515d4b585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{83.01}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270232} :answer-field/id #uuid "53995c65-618d-55f5-ae00-77480a6ad442" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a7c02465-0f29-5382-84ca-baeafb02cecc" :question/math-academy-id "q-140444" :question/problem "Rounded to two decimal places, what is the acute angle between the vectors $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $w=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}?$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{v\\cdot w}{‖v‖\\,‖w‖},\n$$\n\nwe have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{{v}_{1}{w}_{1}+{v}_{2}{w}_{2}+{v}_{3}{w}_{3}+{v}_{4}{w}_{4}}{\\sqrt{{v}_{1}^{2}+{v}_{2}^{2}+{v}_{3}^{2}+{v}_{4}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{w}_{1}^{2}+{w}_{2}^{2}+{w}_{3}^{2}+{w}_{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 4+0\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{5}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{30}\\cdot \\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{6\\sqrt{30}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{30}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{30}})≈{83.01}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272091 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f3622ad-2ea7-574a-aba7-bff64babf1a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 80 \\\\ -15\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269530} {:answer/id #uuid "43bbbbe9-87fc-50fa-9ca4-b69afa75a220" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 80 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269879} {:answer/id #uuid "5b93054f-5f47-5224-85f7-c46ce6379220" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 80 \\\\ -15\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270083} {:answer/id #uuid "8b092faf-0a95-559c-b8a5-48878444af17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270473} {:answer/id #uuid "ecd8b2c9-59cf-55e7-b944-556a08fbae80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 76 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43bbbbe9-87fc-50fa-9ca4-b69afa75a220" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 80 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269879} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b57b72a-6d70-5a47-acab-9a518faa3f1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b4e6c792-0bf4-5c85-ad37-7fcab89a66aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-140446" :question/problem "$$\nB={\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}},\\,[T]_{B|C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 30\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ (shown above) and a basis $C$ of ${R}^{3}.$\nThe matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the linear map $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to these bases, and the vector $x\\in {R}^{2},$ are also given. Find $[T(x)]_{C}.$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the coordinates of the image of $x$ under $T$ relative to the basis $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find $[x]_{B},$ the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B.$ So, let\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 30\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2} &=-15 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2} &=30.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now row-reduce the corresponding augmented matrix using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 & -15 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 30\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 30\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 15\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 20 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 80 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272125 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02cd50ee-7dfb-5329-b414-a7e29e6d91af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269294} {:answer/id #uuid "0e3ed43b-9eec-5604-965c-4bda6251204f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269393} {:answer/id #uuid "71430654-a5e1-51d5-b25d-4d132a09c4f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270275} {:answer/id #uuid "8b96600f-4cfc-560d-a286-0ee5f9d41773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270478} {:answer/id #uuid "d5cb0b79-a29d-51af-b029-65c79862a96d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271144}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5cb0b79-a29d-51af-b029-65c79862a96d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271144} :answer-field/id #uuid "61209408-2d37-576f-b6f0-97778128a9a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "915cd440-9e70-527f-ae9d-6fd020966a43" :question/math-academy-id "q-140529" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$ \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 3}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $b=2,$ $c=-\\frac{1}{2},$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{2}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}=-4.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272128 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0245ee6f-6eed-595d-8f05-de3a8a337d94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269288} {:answer/id #uuid "0a55a7d4-99df-5c21-8561-6b94985ce44e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269361} {:answer/id #uuid "477fcb1e-4945-5111-8193-a1e04fd1813c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269917} {:answer/id #uuid "5bebba35-09ab-5b75-8274-fdce5263c6f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x-\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270087} {:answer/id #uuid "68b0a43b-11d0-52d9-a265-d5894d01e6ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x-\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a55a7d4-99df-5c21-8561-6b94985ce44e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269361} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c0808a8-74a4-5bb5-83bd-e46fd04336eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1e4eb76b-1e24-5200-b802-18e77326289b" :question/math-academy-id "q-140536" :question/problem "Rewrite $-5{x}^{2}-6x+\\frac{1}{5}$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-5$. So, we take out a factor of $-5$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-5({x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{5}x-\\frac{1}{25})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{5}x-\\frac{1}{25}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}\\cdot x-\\frac{1}{25}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $\\frac{1}{25}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}\\cdot x+{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{25}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{25}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5({x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{5}x-\\frac{1}{25}) & =-5({(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =-5{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+(-5)\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =-5{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272147 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3279f5ec-b5ad-574c-aebf-693bd2fdaa1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269730} {:answer/id #uuid "653c963e-8cbf-558c-a076-0707a4bd7a0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270171} {:answer/id #uuid "7e7e2990-b7e7-5be9-a37d-ee55358620c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270375} {:answer/id #uuid "81fcddb5-5383-56b8-bcf4-c993974f8e45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270392} {:answer/id #uuid "ca767cd0-e2d5-5f15-b839-31ed41909f67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81fcddb5-5383-56b8-bcf4-c993974f8e45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270392} :answer-field/id #uuid "87f091c3-7851-5215-bc6b-9b792d0722c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "00737dc0-f397-573a-80e1-d038ce87150d" :question/math-academy-id "q-140717" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-(\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=(\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} \\\\  & ={x}_{2}+2(0) \\\\  & ={x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272186 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a46dca9-6a77-532b-a621-e5e80789d8e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464156} {:answer/id #uuid "5799ce27-8068-5b2f-8fc8-1f78767a9193" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a46dca9-6a77-532b-a621-e5e80789d8e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464156} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6b70a5b-de1a-57c7-b6ce-d41bca7868cc" :answer-field/key "$h(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465012} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "029a3a0e-cfc6-552a-aa69-8f2a2cb429c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464158} {:answer/id #uuid "518c434f-e88b-53ed-afdc-7cca270b0fc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "029a3a0e-cfc6-552a-aa69-8f2a2cb429c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464158} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f08f511-e51f-5132-9f31-250abcbd76a9" :answer-field/key "$h(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465013} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07badc24-5a5f-5098-9c7b-2981336dbdc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464160} {:answer/id #uuid "0c0605c1-7e2a-5150-b68c-ac31d743ba94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07badc24-5a5f-5098-9c7b-2981336dbdc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464160} :answer-field/id #uuid "d54d83fd-9fa4-552f-9d17-d870629c26c9" :answer-field/key "$h(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a7b393e-0e8e-512c-933e-c05c7c1e88c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-141016" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $h(x),$ defined below?\n\n$$\nh(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, & x<\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ 1, & x=\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ 2x, & x>\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $h(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nFirst, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$h(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$” is false. The function is *not* left-continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ since the left-sided limit is *not* equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}h(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0 \\\\  & \\ne h(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$h(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$” is false. The function is *not* right-continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ since the right-sided limit is *not* equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}h(x) & =2(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi  \\\\  & \\ne h(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$h(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$” is false. For the function to be continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is neither left-continuous nor right-continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272192 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "154e3238-1d23-5b80-ab76-254133391d92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{\\pi /2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269452} {:answer/id #uuid "2027ccb2-9c2f-5e11-a4e5-d0b95567c3dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269537} {:answer/id #uuid "2973c585-6274-5cfc-a07d-fc43b727a8db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269643} {:answer/id #uuid "c099d30b-f002-5454-854f-257fb34b5f02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270975} {:answer/id #uuid "f81fadfa-f7fd-52e4-a734-81c6932ad4e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,{e}^{\\pi /2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c099d30b-f002-5454-854f-257fb34b5f02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270975} :answer-field/id #uuid "21ca7b7b-04d4-53c0-a238-71b89e000f1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d9b01c22-41c9-560d-9b14-7e8abb48dc24" :question/math-academy-id "q-141046" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent line to the polar curve $r={e}^{2\\theta }$ at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ given that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )={e}^{2\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )={e}^{2\\theta }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nWe have,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,{e}^{\\pi /2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi /2}(2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,{e}^{\\pi /2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /4} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,{e}^{\\pi /2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,{e}^{\\pi /2})} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272193 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01370d24-1383-5993-99af-9eec26948899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269270} {:answer/id #uuid "27ab6000-389d-51c9-8b50-f6b291ad32aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269617} {:answer/id #uuid "7873fabe-8603-54d4-9449-920763aad695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270337} {:answer/id #uuid "79e9239e-bcb7-5488-ac30-22a04a7a0a2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270347} {:answer/id #uuid "e3716a02-1092-5cf5-8bf8-255eeafbca7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3716a02-1092-5cf5-8bf8-255eeafbca7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271270} :answer-field/id #uuid "805597dc-c057-5e56-900a-9aee60a8f98e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "175ea2b1-bc5a-5a18-84bf-7ca76c72b0dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-141048" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=2\\theta $  at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ given that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nWe have,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =2+0 \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}-2(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =0-\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /2} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{-\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272199 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9eb1ace-da7e-5399-a3fe-3eca3341dc04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437950} {:answer/id #uuid "8294de83-bfe6-5d3e-907f-46fca51b308f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9eb1ace-da7e-5399-a3fe-3eca3341dc04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437950} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f826ec9-1240-515d-9e72-2dd14068a0b0" :answer-field/key "${f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439004} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "307652cb-8dec-5139-b1b9-8b42d2c243ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437952} {:answer/id #uuid "d0fc6fa1-75d1-54ae-a895-4bd473e2976a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "307652cb-8dec-5139-b1b9-8b42d2c243ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437952} :answer-field/id #uuid "93d26810-7687-5dab-8301-97c0074dc993" :answer-field/key "Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439005} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "430fce2c-320b-5e26-93df-7d20527603a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437954} {:answer/id #uuid "4dee6609-1868-55be-9c34-9e4174a83b33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "430fce2c-320b-5e26-93df-7d20527603a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437954} :answer-field/id #uuid "81855480-1021-5030-8cb7-a5a2db6e6e14" :answer-field/key "The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c4402732-29b4-541b-a6e6-a372fd33bf97" :question/math-academy-id "q-141084" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,x\\ne 0\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement ${f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$ is false. Differentiating $f(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & \\ne -\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement” is false. Substituting $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ and ${y}^{′}=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$ into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & \\ne -\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation” is false. Since statement “Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement” is false, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272208 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "282c0558-4ce8-5a0b-9ecb-c71054b4d633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438004} {:answer/id #uuid "e16713e8-3c99-5127-b858-09f6a836bf7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "282c0558-4ce8-5a0b-9ecb-c71054b4d633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438004} :answer-field/id #uuid "66cce0c8-4d30-5e9d-9a30-1ffdbfa68830" :answer-field/key "$y=-\\frac{1}{x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439031} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ecffcc8-53d2-55a3-902f-3663dafe2076" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438006} {:answer/id #uuid "3ecb31ee-bc9e-50df-bac9-69db3dc932bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438007}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ecb31ee-bc9e-50df-bac9-69db3dc932bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438007} :answer-field/id #uuid "272b3862-8779-5572-983f-8c22da89800f" :answer-field/key "$y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439032} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0b1a108-8e14-51d7-8324-6b5a5d23d1af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438008} {:answer/id #uuid "8c2c0aaa-91fa-53d3-af26-2a7cada1ff1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c2c0aaa-91fa-53d3-af26-2a7cada1ff1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438009} :answer-field/id #uuid "92920a70-1e81-5dc7-b31f-286893c6762f" :answer-field/key "$y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0b4e4e14-2aab-5c49-ba69-c1255e3d6145" :question/math-academy-id "q-141099" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{2y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\frac{1}{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{2(-\\frac{1}{x})}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\ne \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{2(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{x} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true.* Therefore, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{2(\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}} & =-\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true.* Therefore, the function $y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$; $y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272214 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "637a72ce-0d98-52b9-bd74-7454769e1306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270150} {:answer/id #uuid "639d7aa9-6e9c-56a5-93f9-c52aa4f843af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270151} {:answer/id #uuid "65c55cd3-8c13-52a3-a22c-496d7ed37306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270176} {:answer/id #uuid "76ca705e-0bfb-57d2-b037-21e60eec8ae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270317} {:answer/id #uuid "952621dd-e2c2-5a13-b19f-0690051a27fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "952621dd-e2c2-5a13-b19f-0690051a27fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270575} :answer-field/id #uuid "499365bc-c9cb-5bfb-b557-42f54e59d06b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "42cc7718-b801-5574-add3-311d9f7972bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-141111" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-2y\\operatorname{tan}⁡2x,\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\sqrt{2}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =A(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272252 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "410198f2-0a2f-5140-b15b-d661fba8d90a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269857} {:answer/id #uuid "4dd620fa-a012-5626-a2a6-a71a4baee0ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269973} {:answer/id #uuid "9a200b60-92d2-5640-8fd6-75963c4d7505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270625} {:answer/id #uuid "c5ccd34b-4384-567e-8465-a2f21a8716fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271013} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1fe70a-09aa-58da-b2e5-377257be5c8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5ccd34b-4384-567e-8465-a2f21a8716fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271013} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7866c9e-696d-5dc5-99fe-3e202db952a6" :answer-field