x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}$:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272300 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2209d435-198d-515f-b8b4-60c9562f6bdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269560} {:answer/id #uuid "273798d5-f257-5699-8aa9-4f4c2f307f49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269613} {:answer/id #uuid "3692e43f-c9b5-5ffd-8fc6-a562924ff89c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269764} {:answer/id #uuid "7a671224-3dde-5038-92af-e145da2474b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270352} {:answer/id #uuid "8e191cef-1fcc-5bb8-9ab2-aa4afff1bdaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{21}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e191cef-1fcc-5bb8-9ab2-aa4afff1bdaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{21}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270499} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f9eedf2-2e58-52db-8e06-a9079bb28565" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b33c0ce6-8797-5117-a675-fc9bb0069eae" :question/math-academy-id "q-141552" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${x}^{2}-11x+25.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}x+25\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $25$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}x+{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+25\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+25\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{21}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272313 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "139d882d-3cd8-5591-95ab-4f02b364c9e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269444} {:answer/id #uuid "27b5c406-c7ab-5ca1-8538-f5588287699a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269620} {:answer/id #uuid "412dd5fe-943d-5e9f-9270-682fb4738770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269859} {:answer/id #uuid "91c7e265-47ef-51f2-a502-687b1a4fa602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{5}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270539} {:answer/id #uuid "f39b5916-e78a-5378-beb3-0cc0c23fcae0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3\\pm \\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "412dd5fe-943d-5e9f-9270-682fb4738770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269859} :answer-field/id #uuid "05523259-00ab-5145-bcab-f2aec15fcd8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1044f32f-7390-525d-94d0-30c2ecd3e1b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-141829" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-4{x}^{2}+4x=-2.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4}$ is a perfect square.*" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4}={(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{x}^{2}+4x & =-2 \\\\ -4({x}^{2}-x) & =-2 \\\\ -4({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x) & =-2 \\\\ -4({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}) & =-2 \\\\ -4[{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}] & =-2 \\\\ -4[{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}] & =-2 \\\\ -4{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+(-4)(-\\frac{1}{4}) & =-2 \\\\ -4{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+1 & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by subtracting $1$ from both sides and then dividing by $(-4),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+1 & =-2 \\\\ -4{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+1-1 & =-2-1 \\\\ -4{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =-3 \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{(-3)}{(-4)} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\ |x-\\frac{1}{2}| & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272319 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "226ed9c2-7689-523e-8b19-51cec8859457" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269567} {:answer/id #uuid "9496f9be-78ac-5c1d-87d1-26319ba1a036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270570} {:answer/id #uuid "b1108937-3dcd-5a86-b465-a26d9dae5c1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270850} {:answer/id #uuid "b195a681-69dc-577a-9b60-69ec56c83520" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270859} {:answer/id #uuid "bf896826-6cab-51c8-9d11-14de92c9b58c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270964}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9496f9be-78ac-5c1d-87d1-26319ba1a036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270570} :answer-field/id #uuid "9091b3e7-e4fb-59b0-9562-180d1542a3af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271766}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d8e5a761-571d-5609-89c6-2c78742817c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-141974" :question/problem "![](images/2b/2b8bbc9ae6b8bf8c441c8e2b534fb159f8180833e1891baa094f988d6a362117.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $D-B?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}-(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=\\frac{6\\pi }{4}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the tangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $1$ units up. Hence, $D=1.$\n\nTherefore, $D-B=1-(\\frac{2}{3})=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3})+1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272322 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9221fa64-263f-5f48-892d-6f005d8622c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270547} {:answer/id #uuid "bc242c34-ecb5-521e-8789-f0f5cf317508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270926} {:answer/id #uuid "bef6282f-c11b-5526-ae43-2ccc5ecff0a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270959} {:answer/id #uuid "dc55ade1-e420-58fe-a493-94b75da85e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271198} {:answer/id #uuid "e3c047d4-8eac-5a44-873f-1c3609c87a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271273}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9221fa64-263f-5f48-892d-6f005d8622c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270547} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b1caf58-03cb-54bf-8e26-0ed84060a904" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271647}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "85585a6b-0c0e-5963-819c-40902d9e809c" :question/math-academy-id "q-142025" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{4\\pi }{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}-\\sqrt{3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272323 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06722ca7-474e-5595-bf36-153b1775fd3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269327} {:answer/id #uuid "1f9a8511-de08-55eb-a39b-8c1d7fb3526d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269532} {:answer/id #uuid "35238dad-241f-56ad-8d8f-e9b4f0502e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269755} {:answer/id #uuid "e79b8881-98bb-524f-b8e6-1f50afbbbdee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271294} {:answer/id #uuid "eb7b137e-47ec-5070-a46e-9c1151db82e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb7b137e-47ec-5070-a46e-9c1151db82e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271335} :answer-field/id #uuid "c897889a-b632-5109-a8b5-b76c95145861" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "81ad9fc7-9d0e-5199-bbb4-6a03531ef9d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-142026" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{-\\sqrt{3}}{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{-1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272329 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f430bea-8bdc-5c1d-ad5c-0fb423bfc8b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{91}{4}-{(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270254} {:answer/id #uuid "7d098b28-9b36-5a5a-a306-848196bbfbd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}-{(y+\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270364} {:answer/id #uuid "c20ec7e5-a2f5-52cc-b689-4b8b3c3e13ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{91}{4}-{(y+\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270986} {:answer/id #uuid "f5575521-acc6-52d8-8ef0-33c9633f2919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{109}{4}-{(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271424} {:answer/id #uuid "f7f0d581-4e01-550f-8515-51c1277eb311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}-{(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7f0d581-4e01-550f-8515-51c1277eb311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}-{(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271443} :answer-field/id #uuid "05fb4a22-6bc6-599f-9a3e-03db139590ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271513}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "801928f5-f859-5bec-ad10-ef9e1c04ab4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-142071" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({y}^{2}-11y+30)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-11y+30\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}y+30\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $30$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}y+{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({y}^{2}-11y+30) & =-({(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-{(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272331 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "090d142f-f7f9-5a7b-b72e-52a078df1eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269350} {:answer/id #uuid "1d201ccd-85fb-549a-9e9c-531394fc50aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269507} {:answer/id #uuid "76ee6731-902a-5dee-b46b-c4380fed56c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270319} {:answer/id #uuid "b75c049c-a748-56a4-b205-01a20869b559" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270893} {:answer/id #uuid "c3f5c1f2-a0ab-5100-8db9-3071ee8511c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76ee6731-902a-5dee-b46b-c4380fed56c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270319} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8980b78-b788-5d75-b585-9a0b63595d13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a1de0aea-4bea-5c0c-94bb-b2903c951763" :question/math-academy-id "q-142142" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{210}^{∘})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v={210}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{210}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{210}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{210}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272339 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39b740b5-03e6-52a1-8778-bb3d5a4643d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269793} {:answer/id #uuid "3b972a24-5c70-5eae-9d0c-716d33795b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269812} {:answer/id #uuid "483860b4-2eb2-5833-8723-a77620ce5853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269925} {:answer/id #uuid "8d8613fe-ae9c-573e-9a33-0b83852831ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270494} {:answer/id #uuid "ac81f94e-33e1-514c-88bb-fa409515889a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b972a24-5c70-5eae-9d0c-716d33795b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269812} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5f6e6d2-08b8-5639-bf32-ba297bae54b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4c02ea3-6977-53a9-ae04-f813c6f1d08f" :question/math-academy-id "q-142369" :question/problem "![](images/40/40375a9794d9e399327cfcf128e017f05d20319fd86d41a8a9f49e01f58ccae0.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=4$  (shown above) at $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =7\\pi /4} & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-(-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272340 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b7994b6-6648-5a65-a26c-70c5f7fb2f57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269494} {:answer/id #uuid "487ea5f2-1698-5061-a1a8-02060dfa49dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269931} {:answer/id #uuid "597732ae-0c0e-5b93-b215-a1c802027f3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270063} {:answer/id #uuid "8f3ef077-3349-5d85-9486-332795fac642" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270517} {:answer/id #uuid "a8142e72-74aa-5580-a47d-81c3b859f781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270771}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "597732ae-0c0e-5b93-b215-a1c802027f3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270063} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f191551-38dd-5366-bc59-27f6a39d541b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271652}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4ae6bdd5-5860-50ce-b445-12c73919d32f" :question/math-academy-id "q-142382" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=12$ at $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=12,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =2\\pi /3} & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275103 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01b271c8-930d-55d2-b6c9-4d10415d1f40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272398} {:answer/id #uuid "453026c0-09cc-5849-a51c-b0765a6a347c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272980} {:answer/id #uuid "7f4bfc4f-7d7a-5012-b1f2-ad8906f9fa0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273481} {:answer/id #uuid "a9c5c442-efee-51ea-b4c4-619423884639" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273854} {:answer/id #uuid "dcd30bde-3736-5aa2-a5e8-1c3be5c7c83c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01b271c8-930d-55d2-b6c9-4d10415d1f40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272398} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bfbd7fa-8ea2-5c3c-b15b-9c490e6cfc9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c2219a64-4fca-5417-a4ef-4988c9db8d90" :question/math-academy-id "q-14310" :question/problem "Find the specific solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+1$ given that  $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(y) & =x+C \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+C).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+C).\n$$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1$ to find $C$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+C) \\\\ \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ C & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+1$ that satisfies the condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275106 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38a63a78-d8f8-520d-9e1a-a547cbc802ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}})+2v)\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272882} {:answer/id #uuid "5798a05b-9e8b-5365-ba0d-17eaf8a8607b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})+v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273138} {:answer/id #uuid "610c7517-5cb8-53dc-8041-977fdbde02dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}})+2v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273226} {:answer/id #uuid "6dd95ac9-8774-563b-bda6-a758e1302a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})+2{v}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273341} {:answer/id #uuid "df13e973-3a12-5311-937b-d3dcae4d2226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}}))\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "610c7517-5cb8-53dc-8041-977fdbde02dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}})+2v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273226} :answer-field/id #uuid "15ca98bc-dec3-575a-b42c-45d374e930cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b4e19450-4e51-548d-b019-a1f8398b116f" :question/math-academy-id "q-14314" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}})+\\frac{3y}{x}.\n$$\n\nIf the transformation $y=v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?" :question/worked-solution "Using the transformation $y=vx$ (where $v=v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}})+\\frac{3y}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{v}^{2}{x}^{2}})+\\frac{3vx}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}})+3v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}})+2v \\\\ (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}})+2v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275111 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d4875ea-48e5-5361-a8c6-15e5b78c5e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272504} {:answer/id #uuid "3ee30a5d-c60d-51de-8718-366ce9c6d7be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x+y}{y})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272925} {:answer/id #uuid "6437d2a5-9b98-5547-b4b2-8979038ea84d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{y}^{2}+xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273247} {:answer/id #uuid "77c9d0da-c801-5a3a-a296-9ff45087e3bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273408} {:answer/id #uuid "89bcafdd-55e6-5e96-9617-87e8ef808b61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6437d2a5-9b98-5547-b4b2-8979038ea84d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{y}^{2}+xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273247} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5a0fc63-6ea7-5103-a6b4-659ab0b835cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274926}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a9372b90-e355-5e25-8427-4c0e772f932c" :question/math-academy-id "q-14322" :question/problem "Which of the following is NOT a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if it satisfies the following property:\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=f(x,y)\\,\n$$\n\nThe only option in which this is NOT the case is:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{y}^{2}+xy}\n$$\n\nWe can check this using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nLet $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{y}^{2}+xy}.$\n\nWe see that the numerator is NOT a homogeneous polynomial. Therefore, $f(x,y)$ is NOT a homogeneous function.\n\nHence, the original differential equation is  NOT homogeneous.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo check this in detail using the definition, we first write:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{y}^{2}+xy}\\,\n$$\n\nWe can check that this is not homogeneous showing that $f(tx,ty)\\ne f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\nFor $x=1$, $y=2$ and $t=3$, for instance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =f(2\\cdot 1,2\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =f(2,6) \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{2}+1}{{6}^{2}+2(6)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{48}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =f(1,2) \\\\  & =\\frac{{1}^{2}+1}{{2}^{2}+1(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, there exists at least one combination of values of $x$, $y$ and $t$ for which the identity $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y)$ is not observed.\n\nHence, the original differential equation is  NOT homogeneous."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275122 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35381417-ae96-547a-8f09-c661f2a6392b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272859} {:answer/id #uuid "47db2918-1d4f-5d72-9867-d0717a28dfd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273001} {:answer/id #uuid "6403670f-9d16-5021-ae53-4275547be540" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273245} {:answer/id #uuid "74dc3b36-73c9-54de-8452-14e52f6d4c12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273385} {:answer/id #uuid "ed688b95-443d-59f5-be09-dc3f86780692" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed688b95-443d-59f5-be09-dc3f86780692" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274488} :answer-field/id #uuid "34f320ed-c938-5762-b511-cc788825f43f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c15605a0-fccc-5892-a3b5-02a0bd086a37" :question/math-academy-id "q-143638" :question/problem "![](images/11/112ee04f02087fbf9f477a4cc002a7b24f7881e1ac2e37a1566ccc43e948cf0a.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{4}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\pi }=2.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the tangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $2$ unit down. Hence, $D=-2.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=2+(-2)=0.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)-2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275123 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d117b4c-f410-531d-89d3-d240d2770c1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272501} {:answer/id #uuid "4d8916c1-d8cc-539d-8500-05fc10ff1e66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273036} {:answer/id #uuid "79c5cd52-cb65-55b4-acff-be2a9c77bec7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273433} {:answer/id #uuid "bc4377ee-1f77-5413-9810-dbe2141bcc16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{17}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274016} {:answer/id #uuid "e30fc5a2-00c2-5877-9a02-34776a22449c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79c5cd52-cb65-55b4-acff-be2a9c77bec7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273433} :answer-field/id #uuid "5721fa62-48d4-5b2c-87d7-f6231add9a93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "39eb902a-b87c-5ca3-bc9b-ac7146c8f3fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-143665" :question/problem "![](images/39/39d3bd73ac0f60f21cc9e71cbf46d62901fcc07cab3b06cd84a496b1e1415a1a.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $5$ units down. Hence, $D=-5.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\frac{3}{2}+(-5)=-\\frac{7}{2}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2})-5.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275124 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ad738c6-8a32-5961-b539-3698f4e9cdf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273021} {:answer/id #uuid "50259aca-1ec1-5fc2-bc96-3ca3f1858648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273065} {:answer/id #uuid "71aa11bf-864b-5f96-989e-b92b337377a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273363} {:answer/id #uuid "dee2a307-86c3-5f67-84cc-ac5b0ed4f0f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274351} {:answer/id #uuid "ff038ed4-d456-5bbd-b444-2bac21ae9a18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274629}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dee2a307-86c3-5f67-84cc-ac5b0ed4f0f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274351} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9af95c9-9911-57b3-8b64-ba864ef27435" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "24086e6f-d631-5bda-bb74-7b003ee42cee" :question/math-academy-id "q-143666" :question/problem "![](images/14/148ef6b6d54262df159703437a6003e8f884052b98e95eb61536890a987a07f1.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B-D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{4\\pi }{5}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{4\\pi }{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{4\\pi }{5})}=\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $\\frac{9}{4}$ units up. Hence, $D=\\frac{9}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, $B-D=\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{9}{4}=-1.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{5x}{4})+\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275125 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b569561-dd6a-56fe-9bd4-68e9ac673886" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273170} {:answer/id #uuid "6ee36efa-131f-5155-a5b2-c1679185c3dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273350} {:answer/id #uuid "a268efae-7964-5246-bfea-d43fd33e4816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273793} {:answer/id #uuid "c4e6e28d-d15c-52b3-aa11-573250fde94a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274093} {:answer/id #uuid "e92cf54e-2ca8-575a-a3ad-f3bb68f26116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ee36efa-131f-5155-a5b2-c1679185c3dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273350} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8f9e0d5-a165-5d96-b7ec-3b0afc3b50ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bc1adc45-4e2f-5dc9-9f7c-79b8e4bcae44" :question/math-academy-id "q-143668" :question/problem "![](images/f6/f6ee30d5c189c9579a1f38d59f06581a7cfff944d42489eab2e3c0c6ce089ad4.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{5\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})}=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $\\frac{12}{5}$ units down. Hence, $D=-\\frac{12}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\frac{3}{5}+(-\\frac{12}{5})=-\\frac{9}{5}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3x}{5})-\\frac{12}{5}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275126 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27dd6c2e-56f6-5224-a92d-a900e47fbf5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272740} {:answer/id #uuid "4d20665a-d072-5d38-bc2e-83cd6ab4dfd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273035} {:answer/id #uuid "69b8a942-cc3f-5887-9b9a-88358ec6410d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273298} {:answer/id #uuid "a7e2122f-6484-54b6-b0a8-b983751ab3ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273841} {:answer/id #uuid "dc531f8c-0ec3-5820-905b-629562f0fa99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27dd6c2e-56f6-5224-a92d-a900e47fbf5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272740} :answer-field/id #uuid "b21fc2a6-944a-50a1-a0fa-69bde4da9e7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274944}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bdfc61bf-0fdc-5699-9c08-0b5a229a8b71" :question/math-academy-id "q-143670" :question/problem "![](images/c2/c2e5b01cf03f1f9d82562ee51fc484448e90b74dd2584daf18a499849ca36bff.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}(x+3C))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+3C$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}(x+3C))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/29/29346a073d6a22f0bab74eeac86431e8c761c9ed21a005ec6965f71e6436c117.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})$ by $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n3C=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{12}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $-\\frac{\\pi }{12}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275128 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fb8c6d5-62f2-5f9e-9dff-a6c6cf210773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272526} {:answer/id #uuid "3d8111fb-6012-56e0-a9b6-3275417d9a31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272916} {:answer/id #uuid "8340b495-0e27-5ab2-8265-89b8a8f42f44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273514} {:answer/id #uuid "ceceedd5-348a-5f86-9a42-9b4e036292b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274185} {:answer/id #uuid "ed397ac8-10b1-54c0-9046-d661bdad85ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d8111fb-6012-56e0-a9b6-3275417d9a31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272916} :answer-field/id #uuid "b42dc950-3ab6-53ee-bb79-c0cd1d57e61e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "587d6141-4c7d-5ff0-9ea2-3cc64d30a411" :question/math-academy-id "q-143672" :question/problem "![](images/6a/6a90f799d2dfd2dbd971e15eb195f4109ab7b6ceb204943a8ed2fd6c83bb620c.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}(x+2C))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+2C$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}(x+2C))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=cot\\,(\\frac{x}{2}).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/0e/0e94fbec60ae4d47d3a4fba50f8a875d9bb3938776940cc9688413d2cb22aa9e.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ units to the left. Therefore:\n\n$$\n2C=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275131 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03bc168c-f11d-51c2-bcec-43e2f2c594d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272418} {:answer/id #uuid "1d2a9ebc-fa26-5fa6-bfe6-f8b5ba052e2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272646} {:answer/id #uuid "24d9abd2-8f95-581f-a159-19f44168c652" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272710} {:answer/id #uuid "af303134-44b2-56cd-975a-36c2a314c7d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273901} {:answer/id #uuid "d30c5ce1-6aae-525e-9853-2bf0d012d50c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af303134-44b2-56cd-975a-36c2a314c7d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273901} :answer-field/id #uuid "0189c09e-e677-5254-a0fb-54a417d40114" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b8a997a9-7fce-538b-be7b-e8029e694287" :question/math-academy-id "q-143676" :question/problem "![](images/0f/0f994407d304e5b1105aeef37a62d6d51bcad43e76757846e0b42b8f257024b3.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2x}{5}+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2x}{5}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2}{5}(x+\\frac{5C}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{5C}{2}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2}{5}(x+\\frac{5C}{2}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2x}{5}).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/1b/1b3e3365d8047befbe70c2371a44e61262c68a2da694ab410ae974917e8535bd.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2x}{5})$ by $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}-\\frac{5\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5C}{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,C=(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})(\\frac{2}{5})\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{5}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $-\\frac{\\pi }{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275132 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38f5df54-de20-581a-a052-687662bac099" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272886} {:answer/id #uuid "59ea7cd8-2c89-5af6-9951-0c58c07e0a6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273157} {:answer/id #uuid "98bf869f-24f8-50f3-ac5e-4060bb11e000" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273712} {:answer/id #uuid "b3d9faeb-5941-52e8-928f-04d747d92a64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273945} {:answer/id #uuid "c58db38f-c8f1-5754-b80e-2298972c7447" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274101}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59ea7cd8-2c89-5af6-9951-0c58c07e0a6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273157} :answer-field/id #uuid "20a99701-dbbe-534f-a0ce-166bf09e58cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7239e789-03d0-5ec3-8e95-e5eb72761b2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-143677" :question/problem "![](images/76/7602d1695610361f28613d8bf3542056b2624f9b6b8980873f6fdebf802c6750.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3}+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}(x+\\frac{3C}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{3C}{2}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}(x+\\frac{3C}{2}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3}).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/01/015ca9336646a7545de842414ace2c2a695688e30653b2ed019000d69609d56d.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3})$ by $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ units to the left. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3C}{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,⟹\\,C=(\\frac{\\pi }{4})(\\frac{2}{3})\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275139 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08a941d2-0004-538c-b3cd-647a6020db00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{43}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272461} {:answer/id #uuid "4023d5d3-1807-53c8-a6c0-65921408fc12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272937} {:answer/id #uuid "5225563d-48bb-51d5-a198-0d482b4970d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{77}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273084} {:answer/id #uuid "cc0df09b-c1ec-5de9-a816-049ff257188c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{36}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274163} {:answer/id #uuid "ee7c6684-b943-55f7-bd43-f70946609a65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{84}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5225563d-48bb-51d5-a198-0d482b4970d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{77}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273084} :answer-field/id #uuid "7941d771-580d-5eea-a4dc-414c95ace302" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a78bd195-69bb-5aa4-8a43-6887179edeb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-143698" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{3}{5},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{8}{17},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{15}{17}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, find the value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v).$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡u=1-{sin}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{9}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{9}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{5})(-\\frac{15}{17})-(\\frac{4}{5})(\\frac{8}{17}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{45}{85}-\\frac{32}{85} \\\\  & =-\\frac{77}{85}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275146 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11d1e3af-673e-5022-9b2a-5ad47d78fa1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{62}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272548} {:answer/id #uuid "16f38d96-a581-5e12-98b5-2f01bfd0e191" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{55}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272588} {:answer/id #uuid "95445e9e-3065-5458-a02b-467a8ec54b12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{60}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273686} {:answer/id #uuid "bd815b00-70e3-5d15-803c-27a16e2daf8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274029} {:answer/id #uuid "f6ea886c-5136-554b-9c06-04935009d10b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{55}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95445e9e-3065-5458-a02b-467a8ec54b12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{60}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273686} :answer-field/id #uuid "472e908f-f6eb-5a41-a91b-ba4f93a91b00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "31c796af-b808-5bf3-a4ec-dfa63a97d44e" :question/math-academy-id "q-143876" :question/problem "Three baskets, $A,B,$ and $C,$ contain pears. It is known that $\\frac{1}{6}$ of the pears from basket $A$ and $\\frac{1}{5}$ of those from basket $B$ are ripe. A basket is selected at random, and a pear is chosen at random from the basket. If the probability that the selected pear is ripe is $\\frac{1}{4},$  what proportion of the pears from basket $C$ are ripe?" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the event that the pear is ripe, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the pear comes from basket $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the pear comes from basket $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the pear comes from basket $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability of selecting a ripe pear, we can use the law of total probability: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R\\cap A)+P(R\\cap B)+P(R\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|B)P(B)+P(R|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since a basket is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=P(B)=P(C)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability of picking a ripe pear from baskets $A$ and $B$ are $\\frac{1}{6}$ and $\\frac{1}{5},$ respectively, we have\n\n$$\nP(R|A)=\\frac{1}{6},\\,P(R|B)=\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\n- Since the probability of picking a ripe pear from one of the baskets is $\\frac{1}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(R|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|B)P(B)+P(R|C)P(C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =(\\frac{1}{6})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{1}{5})(\\frac{1}{3})+P(R|C)(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{18}+\\frac{1}{15}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(R|C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(R|C) & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{18}-\\frac{1}{15} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(R|C) & =\\frac{23}{180} \\\\ P(R|C) & =\\frac{23}{60}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{23}{60}$ of the pears from basket $C$ are ripe."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275148 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a344ed7-da9c-57a0-af2a-71951270f044" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272764} {:answer/id #uuid "45172f34-9a52-5299-830e-7d4b21517809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{44}{125}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272979} {:answer/id #uuid "56eb90ac-0ae9-554d-b1cf-a0e6ac4b635f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273131} {:answer/id #uuid "8317d80f-7e82-5091-8159-51e2495412d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{37}{125}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273512} {:answer/id #uuid "c08ab04f-2c08-5746-b7ca-17c82640fef3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{117}{125}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274057}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45172f34-9a52-5299-830e-7d4b21517809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{44}{125}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272979} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4f65c0b-9e4b-52d4-9016-5e4d942c0430" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1688d8c0-f85a-5a6b-9eed-e20e5ffca899" :question/math-academy-id "q-144398" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{7}{25},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=\\frac{3}{5},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=-\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $x$ lies in the fourth quadrant, find the value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡x=1-{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡x & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{7}{25})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(-7)^{2}}{{25}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{49}{625} \\\\  & =\\frac{625}{625}-\\frac{49}{625} \\\\  & =\\frac{576}{625}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{576}{625}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{24}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x$ lies in the fourth quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{24}{25}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{24}{25})-(-\\frac{4}{5})(-\\frac{7}{25}) \\\\  & =\\frac{72}{125}-\\frac{28}{125} \\\\  & =\\frac{44}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275155 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06850c47-fcbc-5abd-b7d6-b11674ea47ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{3}{2}),$ radius: $\\frac{33}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272447} {:answer/id #uuid "3a9ee61e-e69d-50e4-8aca-53894b381f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{3}{2}),$ radius: $\\sqrt{\\frac{33}{2}}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272897} {:answer/id #uuid "3e54b76c-e1f0-5d14-ab10-98399e5e8200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2}),$ radius: $\\sqrt{\\frac{33}{2}}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272920} {:answer/id #uuid "3ea670b2-8faf-521a-a46b-20ea49b05bdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-\\frac{5}{2},-\\frac{3}{2}),$ radius: $\\sqrt{\\frac{33}{2}}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272922} {:answer/id #uuid "ebd9899c-a884-5944-9dee-f91e9f53b32f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{1}{2}),$ radius: $\\frac{33}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a9ee61e-e69d-50e4-8aca-53894b381f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{3}{2}),$ radius: $\\sqrt{\\frac{33}{2}}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272897} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ab20c73-c972-571f-807c-633d199a2fbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1aecc5c6-9284-53c0-a6bc-d071019a3ece" :question/math-academy-id "q-14620" :question/problem "Find the radius and center of the circle whose equation is\n${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+5x-3y-8=0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+5x-3y-8 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+5x)+({y}^{2}-3y) & =8 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}x+{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}y+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}) & =8+{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}x+{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}y+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}) & =\\frac{33}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect squares and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{33}{2} \\\\ {(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{\\frac{33}{2}})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(-\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{3}{2})$ and its radius is $\\sqrt{\\frac{33}{2}}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275157 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b6e0163-a0ef-5dd5-8824-5a6987c72149" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.75$ square units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272773} {:answer/id #uuid "448a2c20-6c48-5f09-b09b-cd3e42744ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1.38$ square units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272970} {:answer/id #uuid "532b1947-9a20-57d0-9fdc-b0e13f4420bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5.5$ square units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273095} {:answer/id #uuid "6c934433-e0b2-563a-ac51-cb0d21af8ee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.32$ square units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273325} {:answer/id #uuid "e1e310f4-2de7-50e1-93d0-d1dda6e10662" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7.56$ square units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1e310f4-2de7-50e1-93d0-d1dda6e10662" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7.56$ square units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274383} :answer-field/id #uuid "f870ac3c-69e2-5f89-9e4c-ba70a01e7f84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e91a4901-be61-5b18-9885-2ee7e8073da5" :question/math-academy-id "q-14630" :question/problem "A rectangle has a perimeter of $11$ units. What is the largest possible area of the rectangle, rounded to two decimal places?" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: Let us call $x$ and $y$ the dimensions of the rectangle.\n\n![](images/be/bebb7c4ca5e38d75136146f6e4547b0e72d529ce24453a3d0212bca1ffbfe4c0.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such as the area\n\n$$\nA=xy\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 3**: This is subject to the constraint that the perimeter is $11.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\n2x+2y=11.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+2y & =11 \\\\ y & =\\frac{11}{2}-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=\\frac{11}{2}-x$ in the expression $A=xy$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =x(\\frac{11}{2}-x) \\\\ A & =\\frac{11}{2}x-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${A}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(x)=\\frac{11}{2}-2x,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{11}{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{11}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=\\frac{11}{4}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(x)=-2\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(\\frac{11}{4})=-2<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(\\frac{11}{4})$ is negative, we conclude that $x=\\frac{11}{4}$ is indeed a maximum of $A(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=\\frac{11}{4}$, we have \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{11}{2}-\\frac{11}{4}=\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the largest area are $x=\\frac{11}{4}$ and $y=\\frac{11}{4},$ and we conclude that\n\n$$\nA=xy={(\\frac{11}{4})}^{2}≈7.56.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275167 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11ceb279-b498-5112-be99-e2c7cd035a71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\,-\\frac{4}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272547} {:answer/id #uuid "2b7e4790-ea4f-51e5-bc2d-de3c1f027829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\,\\frac{4}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272776} {:answer/id #uuid "2d8c06dc-25a2-5876-97fd-28d301745799" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\,\\frac{1}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272791} {:answer/id #uuid "301acec7-8427-545f-ab2b-e5f848335317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272815} {:answer/id #uuid "ee1f6a3c-5880-5001-b1f4-a6e54abf9363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\,-\\frac{1}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b7e4790-ea4f-51e5-bc2d-de3c1f027829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\,\\frac{4}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272776} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ce63663-348f-55d2-838d-fea135c9c3a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274739}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20636dab-8594-514a-8d86-b7740f06ab8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-14680" :question/problem "The equation of a curve is $2{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}=8.$ The tangent line at the point $(1,\\,1)$ on the curve intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the tangent line at the point $(1,\\,1).$  \n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 4x+12y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 12y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-4x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{4x}{12y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x}{3y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,1)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(1,\\,1)} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{x}{3y})}_{(1,\\,1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the tangent at the point $(1,1)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-{y}_{1}) & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ (y-1) & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x-1)+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this tangent line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x-1)+1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}(0-1)+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,\\frac{4}{3}).$"}]
